= National and International Collaborations

#cv-list(
  [#strong[Auto AI-Pandemics: Democratizing Machine Learning for the Analysis, Study, and Control of Epidemics and Pandemics (2024 – Present)], research initiative funded by the International Development Research Centre (IDRC) under the #emph[AI for Global Health] program, in partnership with UK International Development, with total funding of \$362,500 CAD. The project involves a multidisciplinary collaboration among the University of São Paulo, Fundação Getulio Vargas, University of Brasília (UnB), and Federal University of Technology – Paraná (UTFPR) in Brazil; the University of Leipzig and the Helmholtz Centre for Environmental Research (UFZ) in Germany; and Queen’s University and the London School of Hygiene and Tropical Medicine in the United Kingdom.],
  [#strong[NFDI4Microbiota (2024 – Present)], consortium of the German National Research Data Infrastructure (NFDI) dedicated to the sustainable management, standardization, and sharing of microbiological and microbiome-related research data. The initiative serves as the central national hub for microbiology research data in Germany, supporting the community with FAIR, open, and AI-ready data, metadata standards, analysis services, and training. The consortium is driven by 10 core partner institutions and supported by over 50 participating organizations, encompassing leading universities and non-university research centers, and contributes to the broader One NFDI vision by developing interconnected, cross-disciplinary data infrastructures that accelerate data-driven discovery in microbiology.],
  [#strong[AI MareExplore (2025 – Present)], interdisciplinary research initiative focused on the development of AI-driven microbial bioprospecting pipelines for the sustainable exploitation of marine microbial biocatalysts toward a blue and circular bioeconomy. The project leverages large-scale public marine genomic datasets to train AI language models for biocatalyst prediction across multiple application domains, including carbon capture and plastic waste revalorization. Participating centers include the Helmholtz Centre for Environmental Research (UFZ), GEOMAR Helmholtz Centre for Ocean Research Kiel, Forschungszentrum Jülich (FZJ), and GFZ Helmholtz Centre for Geosciences, with strong integration into international, EU, and national research projects ensuring feasibility, scalability, and dissemination.],
